---
import ProjectLayout from "../../layouts/ProjectLayout.astro";
import ProjectTags from "../../components/ProjectTags.astro";
---

<ProjectLayout>
  <article class="space-y-12">
    <header>
      <p class="text-sm text-gray-500 mb-3">UCLA RoMeLa · TOCATL</p>
      <h1 class="text-4xl sm:text-5xl font-bold mb-5">Hexapod</h1>
      <p class="text-lg leading-relaxed text-gray-700">I developed a Python and ROS 2 control and diagnostics stack for TOCATL, an 18-actuator hexapod. The work brings motor communication, calibration, telemetry, behavior planning, and operator controls into a workflow that can be tested offline and commissioned on hardware.</p>
      <ProjectTags />
    </header>

    <figure>
      <video controls muted playsinline preload="metadata" poster="/images/hexapod/simulation.png" class="w-full aspect-video object-contain">
        <source src="/images/hexapod/walk-simulation.mp4" type="video/mp4" />
      </video>
      <figcaption class="mt-3 text-sm text-gray-500">Walking simulation of the TOCATL platform. This clip demonstrates simulated behavior, not a physical walking trial.</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <section class="space-y-4">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold">What I Built</h2>
      <ul class="list-disc pl-5 space-y-3 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <li>Organized motor control, diagnostics, kinematics, and behavior planning into ROS 2 packages with explicit message and command contracts.</li>
        <li>Integrated dual CAN buses with motor identification, calibration handling, feedback logging, and command limits.</li>
        <li>Developed coordinated leg-motion plans, alternating-tripod behavior previews, and expressive motions for an art installation.</li>
        <li>Built an operator dashboard for selecting motion scope, previewing trajectories, and inspecting controller state before execution.</li>
        <li>Added hardware-independent tests and no-CAN simulation checks, alongside staged motor and leg commissioning tools.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <figure>
      <img src="/images/hexapod/operator-preview.png" alt="TOCATL dashboard showing motion selection and controller state in preview mode" class="w-full h-auto" width="1280" height="720" loading="lazy" />
      <figcaption class="mt-3 text-sm text-gray-500">Operator interface in preview mode, with motion parameters and the selected leg scope visible before execution.</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <section class="space-y-4">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold">Feedback Before Motion</h2>
      <p class="leading-relaxed text-gray-700">A responding bus is not enough to establish that every commanded joint has valid feedback. The control workflow checks the selected motors, calibration state, and telemetry freshness before permitting motion. Missing or stale feedback becomes an explicit diagnostic condition.</p>
      <p class="leading-relaxed text-gray-700">The software separates behavior planning from hardware access, so the same motion can be inspected and tested without opening a live CAN connection. Logs help distinguish communication failures from calibration and trajectory problems.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="space-y-4">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold">Testing and Current Scope</h2>
      <p class="leading-relaxed text-gray-700">The project includes simulation, telemetry inspection, and supervised commissioning workflows. These provide evidence for individual software and hardware interfaces; they do not establish fleet reliability or unrestricted autonomous walking. Physical operation still depends on the configured motor scope and the current hardware checks.</p>
    </section>
  </article>
</ProjectLayout>
